.TH UPDATEPP 1 "September 2026" "PixelPop" "PixelPop Manual"
.SH NAME
updatePP \- build, flash, publish, and install PixelPop firmware
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B updatePP
[\fB\-f\fR] [\fB\-u\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIdevice\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B updatePP
is a zsh shell function, not a firmware component, that builds the PixelPop
firmware from
.I ~/Desktop/Sketches/Matrix/PixelPop
and, depending on the options given, flashes a board connected over USB,
publishes the compiled binary to the
.B rauls4/PixelPop
GitHub repository, and/or tells one specific board on the network to
download and install the currently published build over Wi\-Fi (an OTA
install).
.PP
It always operates in the PixelPop project directory, regardless of the
current working directory when it is invoked.
.PP
With no options,
.B updatePP
only builds the firmware (\fBpio run\fR) and does nothing else.
.PP
If
.B \-o
is given without \fB\-f\fR or \fB\-u\fR, the build step is skipped
entirely: the named board is simply told to install whatever is already
published on GitHub.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f
Flash the board currently connected over USB (\fBpio run \-t upload\fR)
after building. If the board does not enter the bootloader on its own,
hold BOOT, tap RESET, then release BOOT; see
.I FLASHING.md
pitfall 13 in the project root.
.TP
.B \-u
After building, copy the compiled binary to
.I PixelPop.ino.bin
at the repository root and push it to the
.B rauls4/PixelPop
GitHub repository's
.B main
branch. Only that one binary file is staged; nothing else in the working
tree is committed. If the binary is unchanged since the last push, this
prints a message and skips the commit instead of failing. Either way, it
finishes by printing the
.B updatePP \-o
command to run next.
.TP
.BI \-o " device"
Tell
.I device
(an IP address or a \fB.local\fR hostname) to download and install the
build currently published on GitHub, then display its install progress
as a bar until it reports success or failure. This never touches the
board's own automatic\-update setting; it is a one\-shot, one\-time
install, equivalent to opening that board's home page yourself and
clicking its "Install" button.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns non\-zero if the build, the flash, the git push, or the OTA
install fails, or if the board stops responding mid\-install without
having reported success. A dropped connection after the board reports
.B done
is treated as success, since the board only stops answering because it
is restarting into the new build.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B updatePP
Build only, as a quick syntax and compile check.
.TP
.B updatePP \-f
Build, then flash the USB\-connected board.
.TP
.B updatePP \-u
Build, then publish the binary to GitHub.
.TP
.B updatePP \-fu
Build, flash the USB board, and publish to GitHub in one step.
.TP
.B updatePP \-o pixelpop\-livingroom.local
Install whatever is currently on GitHub onto that one board, without
building anything first.
.TP
.B updatePP \-uo 192.168.6.189
Build, publish to GitHub, and immediately install that freshly\-published
build on that board \- build through install in a single command.
.SH WARNINGS
Never run
.B \-o
unattended or from an automated trigger. Only run it while watching the
board: an interrupted OTA install has bricked a board before (see
.I FLASHING.md
pitfall 8 in the project root). If a board misbehaves after an OTA
install,
.B updatePP \-f
over USB returns it to a known\-good build.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/Desktop/Sketches/Matrix/PixelPop
The project directory
.B updatePP
always operates in.
.TP
.I PixelPop.ino.bin
The published binary, at the repository root, that
.B \-u
updates and
.B \-o
installs.
.TP
.I ~/.zshrc
Where the
.B updatePP
shell function itself is defined.
.SH SEE ALSO
.I UPDATEPP.md
and
.I FLASHING.md
in the project root;
.BR pio (1),
.BR curl (1),
.BR git (1).
